  "query_preview": "..This afternoon...Another round of showers and \nthunderstorms are expected across central and northern Arizona \ntoday. Storms have already initiated this morning across central \nArizona, though activity remains fairly mundane. Coverage is \nexpected to increase gradually through the rest of the afternoon, \nespecially north and east of the Mogollon Rim. \n\nLatest RAP guidance has slightly lower MLCAPE values today, but \nremain around 500 J/Kg across the Four Corners, increasing along a \nnortheast to southwest gradient, to around 1500 J/Kg across Yavapai \nCounty. 0-6 km wind shear looks to remain around 20-30 kts from the \ntrough over the west, however this looks to be decreasing through \nthe day. Therefore, a couple strong to severe thunderstorms look to \nremain a possibility this afternoon, with damaging winds and large \nhail the main threats.\n\nPWATs look slightly lower this morning based on the latest RAP \nanalysis, however, guidance forecasts these values to increase \nthrough the afternoon. PWATs are expected to range from around an \ninch across the northeast to around 1.5 inches south of the Mogollon \nRim. This will lead to very high rainfall rates with any stronger \nstorm, which combined with already saturated soils will lead to an \nenhanced flash flood potential today. \n\nTonight...Much of the hi-res guidance continues to forecast a \ncomplex of storms to move northward out of the Lower Deserts later \nthis evening and overnight. As the MCS moves northward, widespread \nthunderstorms look to continue through the overnight across much of \nnorthern Arizona. 12Z HREF PMM guidance has a 30-50% chance of \nexceeding 1\" in 3 hours with this system across much of Yavapai and \nGila counties overnight, and potentially expending a bit further \nnorthward into Coconino. The HREF also highlights a 10% probability \nfor exceeding 3\" in 3 hours.\n\nThe widespread nature of the coverage and the already saturated \nsoils in some areas will only further enhance the risk. Therefore, an \nelevated risk of flash flooding looks to remain into the overnight \nhours across much of central and potions of northern Arizona. A \nFlash Flood Watch is in effect starting this afternoon and lasting \nthrough Monday night.\n\nMonday...One last day of active monsoonal conditions looks to \ncontinue into Monday. Coverage looks to increase as a shortwave \nmoves through the area from the west, adding upper-level dynamics to \naid in precipitation development. However, some uncertainty does \nremain given the potential for debris clouds from overnight \nconvection. This could potentially result in delayed convective \ninitiation and/or reduced coverage. Nevertheless, the flash flood \nthreat remains, along with potentially a couple strong to severe \nthunderstorms.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...A fast-moving trough looks to move \nthrough Great Basin near the middle of next week. This looks to \ninitially suppress monsoonal moisture further south, with much of \nthe GEFS, EPS, and CMCE member showing a sharp decline in PWATs for \nthe middle of next week. Operational guidance seems to have trended \nfurther westward with Hurricane Karina, thereby limiting the \nnorthward push of the sub-tropical moisture surge along Baja \nCalifornia.\n\nHowever, with another tropical system potentially developing behind \nKarina and troughing remaining over the west, an increase in \nmoisture later in the week remains a possibility. GEFS, EPS, and \nCMCE members remain split on continuing with the downward trend, or \nincreasing PWATs potentially as early as Thursday/Friday. Therefore, \nuncertainty remains high near the end of the forecast package as the \nexact timing and track of the both the trough and potential tropical \ncyclone are crucial.\n\nThe operational guidance does have heights tick upwards slightly, so \na slight warming trend seems likely, but overall we look to remain \nseasonable to slightly cooler than normal temperature wise into next \nweek.",
